  "text": "```verilog\nmodule axis_register #\n(\n    parameter DATA_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter KEEP_ENABLE = (DATA_WIDTH>8),\n    parameter KEEP_WIDTH = ((DATA_WIDTH+7)/8),\n    parameter LAST_ENABLE = 1,\n    parameter ID_ENABLE = 0,\n    parameter ID_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter DEST_ENABLE = 0,\n    parameter DEST_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter USER_ENABLE = 1,\n    parameter USER_WIDTH = 1,\n    parameter REG_TYPE = 2\n)\n(\n    input  wire                   clk,\n    input  wire                   rst,\n    /*\n     * AXI Stream input\n     */\n    input  wire [DATA_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tdata,\n    input  wire [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tkeep,\n    input  wire                   s_axis_tvalid,\n    output wire                   s_axis_tready,\n    input  wire                   s_axis_tlast,\n    input  wire [ID_WIDTH-1:0]    s_axis_tid,\n    input  wire [DEST_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tdest,\n    input  wire [USER_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tuser,\n    /*\n     * AXI Stream output\n     */\n    output wire [DATA_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tdata,\n    output wire [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tkeep,\n    output wire                   m_axis_tvalid,\n    input  wire                   m_axis_tready,\n    output wire                   m_axis_tlast,\n    output wire [ID_WIDTH-1:0]    m_axis_tid,\n    output wire [DEST_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tdest,\n    output wire [USER_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tuser\n);\n\n    // Internal state registers\n    reg [DATA_WIDTH-1:0]   reg_data;\n    reg [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0]   reg_keep;\n    reg                    reg_last;\n    reg [ID_WIDTH-1:0]     reg_id;\n    reg [DEST_WIDTH-1:0]   reg_dest;\n    reg [USER_WIDTH-1:0]   reg_user;\n    reg                    reg_valid;\n    reg                    reg_ready;\n\n    // Control signals\n    wire wire_mode;\n    wire buffer_mode;\n    wire skid_mode;\n\n    assign wire_mode   = (REG_TYPE == 1);\n    assign buffer_mode = (REG_TYPE == 2);\n    assign skid_mode   = (REG_TYPE == 3);\n\n    // Logic to determine if we are in a stall condition\n    // A stall occurs when the register is full (valid) and cannot accept new data\n    // or when the register is empty and cannot output data.\n    // For a single register slice:\n    // - If wire_mode: pass through immediately if ready.\n    // - If buffer_mode: store on valid/ready, output on valid/ready.\n    // - If skid_mode: store on valid/ready, output on valid/ready (same as buffer for single stage).\n    \n    // Determine if the register is currently holding valid data\n    wire data_held = reg_valid;\n\n    // Determine if the register is ready to accept new data\n    // In wire mode, we are always ready if the output is ready.\n    // In buffer/skid mode, we are ready if we are not holding data OR if the output is ready.\n    // However, the standard behavior for a \"register\" in a pipeline is:\n    // Input Ready = (Not Valid) OR (Output Ready)\n    // This ensures data is captured on the next clock edge if the path is clear.\n    \n    // Calculate s_axis_tready\n    // If wire_mode: s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready\n    // If buffer_mode: s_axis_tready = (!reg_valid) | m_axis_tready\n    // If skid_mode: s_axis_tready = (!reg_valid) | m_axis_tready\n    \n    wire next_ready;\n    assign next_ready = (wire_mode) ? m_axis_tready : (!reg_valid | m_axis_tready);\n\n    // Calculate m_axis_tvalid\n    // If wire_mode: m_axis_tvalid = s_axis_tvalid & s_axis_tready\n    // If buffer_mode: m_axis_tvalid = reg_valid & m_axis_tready\n    // If skid_mode: m_axis_tvalid = reg_valid & m_axis_tready\n    \n    wire next_valid;\n    assign next_valid = (wire_mode) ? (s_axis_tvalid & s_axis_tready) : (reg_valid & m_axis_tready);\n\n    // Logic to update internal state\n    // Data is updated on the clock edge if s_axis_tvalid is high and s_axis_tready is high\n    // (i.e., data is accepted)\n    \n    always @(posedge clk or posedge rst) begin\n        if (rst) begin\n            reg_data      <= 0;\n            reg_keep      <= 0;\n            reg_last      <= 0;\n            reg_id        <= 0;\n            reg_dest      <= 0;\n            reg_user      <= 0;\n            reg_valid     <= 0;\n            reg_ready     <= 1; // Output is ready initially\n        end else begin\n            // Update state only if data is accepted (s_axis_tvalid && s_axis_tready)\n            if (s_axis_tvalid && s_axis_tready) begin\n                reg_data      <= s_axis_tdata;\n                reg_keep      <= s_axis_tkeep;\n                reg_last      <= s_axis_tlast;\n                reg_id        <= s_axis_tid;\n                reg_dest      <= s_axis_tdest;\n                reg_user      <= s_axis_tuser;\n                reg_valid     <= 1;\n            end else begin\n                // If not accepting data, keep current state\n                // reg_valid remains as is\n                // Note: In some implementations, if the register is empty and input is invalid,\n                // we might want to ensure reg_valid is 0, but since we only set it to 1 on accept,\n                // it naturally stays 0 if never accepted.\n                // However, if we were in a state where we held data but stopped accepting,\n                // reg_valid stays 1 until we output it.\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // Assign output signals based on mode\n    // Wire Mode: Pass through immediately\n    // Buffer/Skid Mode: Use registered values\n    \n    assign m_axis_tdata      = (wire_mode) ? s_axis_tdata : reg_data;\n    assign m_axis_tkeep      = (wire_mode) ? s_axis_tkeep : reg_keep;\n    assign m_axis_tvalid     = next_valid;\n    assign m_axis_tlast      = (wire_mode) ? s_axis_tlast : reg_last;\n    assign m_axis_tid        = (wire_mode) ? s_axis_tid : reg_id;\n    assign m_axis_tdest      = (wire_mode) ? s_axis_tdest : reg_dest;\n    assign m_axis_tuser      = (wire_mode) ? s_axis_tuser : reg_user;\n\n    // Output ready signal\n    // In wire mode, output ready is simply the input ready.\n    // In buffer/skid mode, output ready is the internal ready signal.\n    // The internal ready signal is driven by the logic: if not valid, we are ready.\n    // If valid, we are ready only if the downstream is ready.\n    // This logic is handled by the state machine implicitly via the 'reg_ready' concept \n    // but for AXI Stream, the 'ready' signal is an output that indicates the register \n    // is prepared to accept data.\n    \n    // Actually, for a single stage register:\n    // s_axis_tready should be asserted if the register can accept data.\n    // It can accept data if it is empty OR if the downstream is ready.\n    // m_axis_tready is not an input to this module, but m_axis_tvalid drives the downstream.\n    // The downstream's tready determines if we can push data.\n    \n    // Re-evaluating s_axis_tready logic for correctness:\n    // If wire_mode: s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready (which is not an input here, wait).\n    // The module interface has m_axis_tready as an INPUT.\n    // So in wire mode, we just pass the input ready to output ready.\n    // In buffer mode, we are ready if we are empty OR if the downstream (m_axis_tready) is ready.\n    \n    assign s_axis_tready = (wire_mode) ? m_axis_tready : (!reg_valid | m_axis_tready);\n\n    // Note on m_axis_tready: It is an input.\n    // The logic above uses m_axis_tready to determine if we can output data (next_valid)\n    // and if we are ready to accept data (s_axis_tready).\n    \n    // One edge case: If reg_valid is 1 and m_axis_tready is 0, we hold data.\n    // s_axis_tready becomes 0.\n    // Next cycle, if m_axis_tready becomes 1, next_valid becomes 1, reg_valid becomes 1 (no change),\n    // but we need to ensure the data moves.\n    // Wait, the state update logic:\n    // if (s_axis_tvalid && s_axis_tready) -> update state.\n    // If s_axis_tvalid is 0 (downstream not ready), we don't update state.\n    // If s_axis_tvalid is 1 and s_axis_tready is 1 (downstream ready), we update state.\n    // BUT, if reg_valid is 1, and s_axis_tvalid is 1, and s_axis_tready is 1:\n    // We update reg_data with new data. This overwrites the old data.\n    // This is correct for a FIFO/Buffer: new data replaces old data if the slot is occupied?\n    // No, a single register slice usually acts as a 1-deep FIFO.\n    // If it is full (reg_valid=1), we should NOT accept new data until we output the old data.\n    // My logic: s_axis_tready = (!reg_valid | m_axis_tready).\n    // If reg_valid=1 and m_axis_tready=0 -> s_axis_tready=0. Correct (cannot accept).\n    // If reg_valid=1 and m_axis_tready=1 -> s_axis_tready=1. Correct (can accept).\n    // If we accept, we overwrite reg_data.\n    // Is this correct?\n    // If reg_valid=1, it means we have data. If we accept new data, we replace it.\n    // Then next cycle, we output the new data.\n    // This effectively implements a \"skid buffer\" or \"buffer\" where the register holds 1 beat.\n    // If the downstream is ready, we push the new data immediately.\n    // If the downstream is not ready, we hold the old data.\n    // This seems correct for a 1-deep buffer.\n    \n    // However, consider the case where s_axis_tvalid is 0.\n    // If reg_valid=1, s_axis_tvalid=0, m_axis_tready=0.\n    // s_axis_tready = 0.\n    // State doesn't change. We hold data. Correct.\n    \n    // If reg_valid=1, s_axis_tvalid=0, m_axis_tready=1.\n    // s_axis_tready = 1.\n    // State doesn't change (because s_axis_tvalid is 0).\n    // We output data (next_valid = 1 & 1 = 1).\n    // Correct.\n    \n    // If reg_valid=0, s_axis_tvalid=1, m_axis_tready=0.\n    // s_axis_tready = 1 (empty).\n    // State doesn't change (s_axis_tready is 1, but s_axis_tvalid is 1? Wait).\n    // Condition: if (s_axis_tvalid && s_axis_tready).\n    // Here s_axis_tvalid=1, s_axis_tready=1.\n    // We update state: reg_valid becomes 1.\n    // We output: next_valid = 0 & 0 = 0.\n    // Correct. We captured data, but couldn't output yet.\n    \n    // If reg_valid=0, s_axis_tvalid=1, m_axis_tready=1.\n    // s_axis_tready = 1.\n    // Update state: reg_valid becomes 1.\n    // Output: next_valid = 1 & 1 = 1.\n    // Correct. Throughput.\n\n    // Final check on parameters:\n    // KEEP_ENABLE, ID_ENABLE, etc. are used to determine widths, but the module assumes\n    // the vectors are sized correctly by the parameters.\n    // If KEEP_ENABLE is 0, KEEP_WIDTH is 1.\n    // If ID_ENABLE is 0, ID_WIDTH is 8 (default).\n    // The code uses the parameters directly for sizing, which is compliant.\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>\n<|endoftext|>",
